  "query_preview": "910 AM...\nMorning debris clouds and perhaps a couple light showers are\nmoving east to west along and near the International Border\nwith TDs around 60. The combination of yesterday's gulf surge\nand recycled moisture from the large MCS that moved south of the\nborder last night has pushed deeper moisture into the area,\nespecially seen on the layered CIRAPW. This will set the stage\nfor another active day. \n\nThe previous discussion summed everything up well, with the \nfocus on areas south and east of Tucson, generally on a line \nfrom Nogales to Benson to Safford, with lesser probabilities \nfrom Sasabe to Tucson north and west. The best chance for the \nTucson Metro will be the far east, south, and southwest portions\nof the city.\n\nThe main threats today will be dust, isolated damaging wind\ngusts to 50-60 mph, and flash flooding. High res CAMs and HREF \nshow high probs of widespread wind gusts greater than 30kts \nwith ~10% of 50 kts. This is pushing us toward greater \nconfidence of more dust issues this afternoon and evening along\nthe I-10 corridor from eastern Pima to the NM border and will \nbe considering issuing a dust adv to account for this signal. \n\nAs for the flash flood threat, the most favorable areas will be \nsouth-central and southwestern Cochise into eastern Santa Cruz\nCOs, where more widespread amounts of 0.25\" to 0.75\" with \nlocalized amounts up to 2\" occurred yesterday evening. HREF and \nother guidance shows 6hr PMM amounts of 0.5 to 1.25\" with \nreasonable higher amounts up to 2.5\". This would easily lead to\nflash flooding if similar areas are hit again.  \n\nTiming of activity will be first across higher elevations \n12-2pm quickly pushing into the lower elevations by late \nafternoon and evening, gradually waning in intensity and exiting\nAZ by late evening.",
